      'The tenth year of prophethood was named Sorrow: Khadijah, the believing wife and financier, died; Abu Talib, the protecting uncle, died; Mecca’s boycott had already starved the clan for three years. The Prophet ﷺ walked to Taif for refuge — and was stoned till his sandals filled with blood, praying the famous oppressed-dua under a vineyard wall. Grief was complete; honour was scheduled.',
      'On a quiet night Jibril arrived, opened his chest, washed his heart with Zamzam water in a golden basin, and sealed it with faith and wisdom. Purification precedes ascension in every journey — hearts are washed before heavens open. Then Buraq was brought: a white steed between mule and donkey, stepping as far as sight reaches, tethered at the ring where prophets tether.',
      'In moments the Haram’s fugitive stood at Al-Aqsa — glory be to Him who carried His servant by night from the Sacred Mosque to the Farthest Mosque, whose surroundings are blessed (17:1). There 124,000 prophets waited — Adam to Isa — and Muhammad ﷺ led them in prayer. The imamate of all prophets, conferred in one congregation, is the ummah’s charter of finality: every prior law honoured, every prior prophet praying behind.',
      'He was offered milk and wine, and chose milk. You chose fitrah, Jibril said — you and your ummah guided aright. Choices in visions carry rulings: purity over intoxication, nature over distortion, morning clarity over nightly escape.',
      'Then the ascent — mi’raj, ladder — heaven by heaven with Jibril seeking entry. First heaven: Adam, greeting his righteous son. Second: Yahya and Isa, maternal cousins welcoming their brother. Third: Yusuf, granted half of beauty. Fourth: Idris, raised to a high station. Fifth: Harun, beloved elder. Sixth: Musa, who wept — a youth after him would bring more followers. Seventh: Ibrahim, leaning on the Frequented House, greeting the best of sons. Each greeting, each dialogue, wove the prophetic family into one household with one heir.',
      'Beyond the seventh came Sidratul-Muntaha — the Lote-tree of the Utmost Boundary, veiled in colours indescribable, where sight did not swerve nor transgress (53:17–18). There the Prophet saw Jibril in true form and received three gifts: the last verses of Baqarah, forgiveness for major sins short of shirk, and fifty daily prayers.',
      'Descending past Musa, he was counselled to seek reduction — your ummah cannot bear fifty. He returned repeatedly till five remained, with the reward of fifty; Musa urged further reduction, but the Prophet answered: I am shy before my Lord. The caller announced: My word is not changed — five in deed, fifty in reward. Mercy negotiated through a prophet’s sympathy still governs every Fajr the ummah prays.',
      'Morning in Mecca brought mockery. He described Aqsa’s doors, windows and caravans in detail — a caravan’s lost camel, its arrival time — and the verifiers confirmed each item. Yet Quraysh laughed. Abu Bakr, told the news, answered without visiting the site: if he said it, it is true — I believe him in farther matters, revelation morning and evening. That sentence named him al-Siddiq, the Confirmer, forever.',
      'The journey compresses theology: body and soul travelled (not dream alone — the mockers demanded physical proofs and received them); prophets are alive in the barzakh; prayer outranks all rites, received in heaven not legislated on earth; Jerusalem binds the ummah’s heart as the first qiblah and the ascension’s gate.',
